timestep_convergence
====================

Whether the energy each coupling method delivers is converged in the timestep, run on the density-jump
problem because it is the one where the gas sub-cycles below the dark matter. MaxSizeTimestep sets the
step of the dark matter, which has no acceleration here, so halving it doubles how often the donors
deposit while the gas stays free to take shorter steps under the Courant condition.

Files
-----
  run_matrix.sh  builds and runs both methods at five timesteps

It borrows the IC and the parameter file from the density_jump problem, so build those first:

  python3 scripts/test_problems/outputs/density_jump/make_ics.py

Running
-------
From the repository root:

  scripts/test_problems/outputs/timestep_convergence/run_matrix.sh 4

Ten configurations, MFM only, about 25 minutes on four tasks. The finest costs sixteen times the
coarsest, since it takes sixteen times as many steps.

Results
-------
Ratio of the energy the gas received to the analytic total the black holes radiated:

  MaxSizeTimestep     receiver       donor      steps
  0.02             0.991350899  0.999824937        65
  0.01             0.991448704  0.999925270       129
  0.005            0.991551756  0.999999994       257
  0.0025           0.991589195  0.999999993       513
  0.00125          0.991598649  0.999999994      1025

Two separate results.

The donor method is exactly conservative in the converged limit. Its ratio reaches 0.999999994 at
MaxSizeTimestep = 0.005 and does not move for two further halvings, so the 7.5e-5 shortfall at 0.01 is
a discretisation error and not a leak in the bookkeeping. Coarsening instead makes it worse in the way
a first-order error should: 7.5e-5 at 0.01, 1.75e-4 at 0.02.

The receiver method converges to a value that is not one. Successive differences are 9.8e-5, 1.03e-4,
3.74e-5 and 9.45e-6, shrinking by about four per halving once the first two are past, which puts the
limit at 0.99160. So its 0.84 per cent shortfall on this problem survives refinement: it is a property
of reconstructing the dark matter density at the gas positions, not of the timestep. Refining the
timestep makes the receiver slightly worse rather than better, because a better-resolved blast
evacuates the injection region more effectively and leaves the peak sampled by fewer, larger cells.

Neither method needs a timestep finer than 0.005 on this problem, which is a fortieth of the sound
crossing time of the heated region at the end of the run.
